===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fro}} [[envoluper]] (modern French: [[envelopper]]), from [[en-]] "in" (from {{etyl|la}} [[in-]]) + [[voloper]] "wrap up" (of uncertain, perhaps Celtic origin)

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{transitive}} To [[surround]] or [[enclose]].

